\section{Appendix D: Hardware Determinism Models}

This appendix provides the complete specification of the hardware determinism
models used within the Unified Invariant Simulation Architecture. Determinism
is a foundational requirement for epistemological certainty: identical
simulation outputs must be produced across all hardware substrates,
regardless of architecture, instruction set, memory hierarchy, or parallel
execution model.

Let $\mathcal{H}$ denote the hardware substrate set. Determinism requires:

\[
\forall h_i, h_j \in \mathcal{H},\quad
\mathrm{Sim}(h_i) = \mathrm{Sim}(h_j).
\]

The following sections detail the deterministic posture enforced across CPUs,
GPUs, distributed clusters, and heterogeneous memory systems.

\subsection{D.1 CPU Determinism Model}

CPU determinism is enforced through:

\begin{itemize}
    \item strict IEEE 754 floating-point compliance,
    \item deterministic PRNG seeding,
    \item reproducible multi-threading (Rayon),
    \item fixed instruction ordering,
    \item elimination of nondeterministic branching.
\end{itemize}

All CPU simulations use:

\begin{itemize}
    \item fixed-width SIMD operations,
    \item deterministic cache-line access patterns,
    \item pinned thread affinity,
    \item stable memory allocation.
\end{itemize}

CPU determinism ensures that identical inputs produce identical outputs across
x86\_64, ARM64, and RISC-V architectures.

\subsection{D.2 GPU Determinism Model}

GPU determinism is enforced through:

\begin{itemize}
    \item reproducible compute shaders (OpenCL/CUDA),
    \item deterministic kernel launch ordering,
    \item fixed warp/block scheduling,
    \item stable memory coalescing,
    \item elimination of race conditions.
\end{itemize}

All GPU simulations use:

\begin{itemize}
    \item deterministic floating-point rounding modes,
    \item fixed thread-block dimensions,
    \item stable shared-memory access patterns,
    \item deterministic reduction operations.
\end{itemize}

GPU determinism ensures identical outputs across NVIDIA, AMD, and Intel GPUs.

\subsection{D.3 Distributed Cluster Determinism Model}

Distributed determinism is enforced through:

\begin{itemize}
    \item deterministic message passing (MPI),
    \item fixed node ordering,
    \item stable network latency compensation,
    \item deterministic load balancing,
    \item reproducible reduction trees.
\end{itemize}

All distributed simulations use:

\begin{itemize}
    \item fixed partitioning of spatial domains,
    \item deterministic halo exchange patterns,
    \item stable checkpointing intervals,
    \item reproducible synchronization barriers.
\end{itemize}

Distributed determinism ensures identical outputs across heterogeneous
clusters, cloud compute environments, and mixed-architecture nodes.

\subsection{D.4 Memory Determinism Model}

Memory determinism is enforced through:

\begin{itemize}
    \item stable allocation patterns,
    \item deterministic pointer arithmetic,
    \item fixed alignment constraints,
    \item reproducible paging behavior,
    \item elimination of nondeterministic heap fragmentation.
\end{itemize}

All memory systems use:

\begin{itemize}
    \item deterministic garbage collection (if applicable),
    \item pinned buffers for solver-critical data,
    \item stable stack-frame layouts,
    \item deterministic cache eviction policies.
\end{itemize}

Memory determinism ensures identical outputs across DDR4, DDR5, HBM, and
unified memory architectures.

\subsection{D.5 PRNG Determinism Model}

Deterministic pseudorandom number generation is enforced through:

\begin{itemize}
    \item fixed PRNG seed,
    \item reproducible sequence generation,
    \item stable branching behavior,
    \item elimination of hardware entropy sources.
\end{itemize}

All PRNG operations use:

\begin{itemize}
    \item deterministic XORShift or PCG sequences,
    \item fixed iteration counts,
    \item stable sampling distributions.
\end{itemize}

This ensures that stochastic perturbations remain identical across hardware.

\subsection{D.6 Unified Deterministic Execution Posture}

The unified deterministic execution posture requires:

\begin{itemize}
    \item identical solver outputs across CPUs, GPUs, clusters, and memory systems,
    \item zero nondeterministic branching,
    \item stable floating-point rounding,
    \item deterministic parallel execution,
    \item reproducible memory access patterns.
\end{itemize}

Let $\mathrm{Sim}(h)$ denote the simulation output on hardware substrate $h$.
Unified determinism requires:

\[
\mathrm{Sim}(h_i) - \mathrm{Sim}(h_j) = 0.
\]

\subsection{D.7 Determinism Stress Tests}

Under maximum-load stress:

\begin{itemize}
    \item CPU thread saturation,
    \item GPU warp divergence,
    \item distributed network congestion,
    \item memory fragmentation,
    \item PRNG overload.
\end{itemize}

Determinism must remain intact:

\[
\forall h_i, h_j \in \mathcal{H},\quad
\mathrm{Sim}(h_i) = \mathrm{Sim}(h_j).
\]

\subsection{D.8 Determinism Sovereignty Condition}

Determinism sovereignty is achieved when:

\[
\text{Reproducibility} = 100\%,\qquad
\text{Drift} = 0,\qquad
\text{Collapse} = 0.
\]

This confirms that the unified system behaves as a single mathematical
organism independent of physical hardware.